\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bishops on Path To Refuse Biden Holy Communion\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,832 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The decision was aimed at the nation's second Catholic president and exposed bitter divisions in American Catholicism.\par \par \uc2 The Roman Catholic bishops of the United States, flouting a warning from the Vatican, have overwhelmingly voted to draft guidance on the sacrament of the Eucharist, advancing a push by conservative bishops to deny President Biden communion because of his support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The decision, made public on Friday afternoon, is aimed at the nation's second Catholic president, perhaps the most religiously observant commander in chief since Jimmy Carter, and exposes bitter divisions in American Catholicism. It capped three days of contentious debate at a virtual June meeting of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops. The measure was approved by a vote of 73 percent in favor and 24 percent opposed.\par \par \uc2 The Eucharist, or holy communion, is one of the most sacred rituals in Christianity, and bishops have grown worried in recent years about declining Mass attendance and misunderstanding of the importance of the sacrament to Catholic life.\par \par \uc2 But the move to target a president, who regularly attends Mass and has spent a lifetime steeped in Christian rituals and practices, is striking coming from leaders of the president's own faith, particularly after many conservative Catholics turned a blind eye to the sexual improprieties of former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  because they supported his political agenda. It reveals a uniquely American Catholicism increasingly at odds with Rome and \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Asked about the bishops' decision at a vaccination event on Friday, Mr. Biden said it was ''a private matter and I don't think that's going to happen.''\par \par \uc2 The action is the latest sign of how the nation's bitter political divisions are shaping religious life. Christians across denominations are facing similar divides. Earlier this week at the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  meeting in Nashville, a more moderate majority narrowly headed off a takeover attempt by a hard-right movement.\par \par \uc2 The text of the proposal itself has not been written and would ultimately require approval by a two-thirds majority vote. The proposed outline, earlier reported by America Magazine, said it would ''include the theological foundation for the Church's discipline concerning the reception of Holy Communion and a special call for those Catholics who are cultural, political, or parochial leaders to witness the faith.''\par \par \uc2 Some conservatives want to use such a statement as theological justification to deny communion to Mr. Biden and Catholic politicians like him who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The decision immediately drew criticism from 60 Catholic Democrats in Congress, who urged the bishops ''to not move forward and deny this most holy of all sacraments'' and who challenged the bishops by outlining their own commitment to ''making real the basic principles that are at the heart of Catholic social teaching.''\par \par \uc2 But the fact that Mr. Biden's views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are even a matter of public discussion is already a victory for conservative Catholics.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden, like \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 , embodies a liberal Christianity focused less on sexual politics and more on racial inequality, climate change and poverty. His administration is a reversal of the power that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, including bishops who advanced the measure, enjoyed under Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 The fight comes as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists across the United States are emboldened and as \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists want Mr. Biden to speak more forcefully in their defense. State legislatures have introduced more than 500 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions over the past five months, and the Supreme Court, with its newly expanded conservative majority, agreed to take up a case on a Mississippi law that bans most abortions at 15 weeks, which could challenge the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established in Roe v. Wade. Five of the court's six Catholic justices were appointed by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The bishops are expected to vote on the forthcoming statement in November, ahead of the midterm elections, giving conservatives a tool to criticize Democratic politicians throughout the campaign cycle. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been one of the most mobilizing political forces for the religious right.\par \par \uc2 That subtext was made plain as the bishops debated the topic for more than two hours on Thursday: ''I can't help but wonder if the years 2022 and 2024 might be part of the rush,'' Bishop Robert M. Coerver of Lubbock, in Texas, said.\par \par \uc2 Bishop Kevin C. Rhoades, who leads the bishops' Committee on Doctrine, which put forward the communion effort, replied in a news conference that the upcoming midterm and presidential elections ''never entered my mind, or the committee's.''\par \par \uc2 The United States Conference of Catholic Bishops, an assembly of the country's 433 active and retired bishops, can issue guideline statements, but it does not have the authority to decide who can or cannot receive the sacrament of communion. That power is reserved for the local bishop, who has autonomy in his diocese, or the Pope.\par \par \uc2 Cardinal Wilton Gregory, the archbishop of Washington and the nation's first African-American cardinal, has made it abundantly clear that he does not support denying communion to Mr. Biden. Bishop-elect William Koenig of Wilmington, Del., Mr. Biden's hometown, has remained largely quiet on the issue ahead of his installation next month.\par \par \uc2 Usually the bishops' annual June meeting is a dry affair. But this week's was the most riveting in years, not only because of the topic but also because it revealed the stark divide, theologically and politically, among the church's U.S. leaders.\par \par \uc2 The vote was technically about drafting a theological statement on the Eucharist, and in part comes from the bishops' long-term strategic plan to address declining Mass attendance and misunderstanding about the sacrament.\par \par \uc2 Bishops grew alarmed about communion in 2019, before Mr. Biden was a front-runner in the presidential race, when a Pew poll found that only about a third of U.S. Catholics believed central Catholic teaching that the communion bread and wine literally becomes the body and blood of Christ during Mass.\par \par \uc2 But debates over two days this week revealed the political contours of the fight, as bishop after bishop defended his corner of a polarized American Catholicism.\par \par \uc2 The meeting opened on Wednesday with a 45-minute debate over whether to even approve the agenda because it would include the controversial vote. A retired bishop, Michael Pfeifer of San Angelo, in Texas, urged the conference to address the ''new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiatives of our president, especially the one about infanticide.'' (Mr. Biden does not support infanticide.) Archbishop Mitchell Rozanski of St. Louis attempted what was effectively a filibuster of the communion discussion entirely.\par \par \uc2 Conservative bishops pressed their case in a more than two-hour debate on Thursday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 ''We've never had a situation like this where the executive is a Catholic president who is opposed to the teaching of the church,'' Bishop Liam Cary of Baker, in Oregon, said.\par \par \uc2 Bishops from places like Tyler, Texas, and Sioux Falls, S.D., argued that the people in their churches wanted bishops to create the communion document. Bishop Donald J. Hying of Madison, in Wisconsin, said he speaks almost daily with Catholics ''who are confused by the fact that we have a president who professes devout Catholicism and yet advances the most radical, pro-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda in our history.''\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Salvatore Cordileone of San Francisco, the home diocese of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , who is a Catholic Democrat, said bishops would not be taken seriously if they did not create the communion document. ''Our credibility is on the line,'' he said. ''The eyes of the whole country are on us right now.''\par \par \uc2 Bishops seen as allies of \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 ' direction for the church pushed back. Bishop Robert W. McElroy of San Diego cautioned that moving forward would make it ''impossible to prevent the weaponization of the Eucharist in partisan battles.''\par \par \uc2 ''Once we legitimate public policy-based Eucharistic exclusion as a regular part of our teaching office -- and that is the road to which we are headed -- we will invite all of the political animosities that so tragically divide our nation into the very heart of the Eucharistic celebration,'' he said. ''That sacrament which seeks to make us one will become for millions of Catholics a sign of division.''\par \par \uc2 When Bishop Joseph J. Tyson of Yakima asked if the debate was focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the exclusion of other issues on which public figures might disagree with the Church's teachings, Bishop Rhoades blamed the publicity for overly focusing on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of other issues like human trafficking and white supremacy.\par \par \uc2 About 56 percent of U.S. Catholics support legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but about two-thirds of Catholics who attend Mass regularly do not, according to a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  survey conducted in March.\par \par \uc2 Catholics in general are divided on party lines over whether Mr. Biden should receive communion: 55 percent of Catholic Republicans think he should be denied communion, and 87 percent of Catholic Democrats think he should not, according to Pew.\par \par \uc2 The tension over Mr. Biden's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies has been growing for months. Shortly after Mr. Biden's election in November, Archbishop Jos\u233\'00\'E9 H. Gomez of Los Angeles, the president of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops, announced the unusual creation of a working group to address conflicts that could arise between his administration's policies and church teaching.\par \par \uc2 On Inauguration Day, Archbishop Gomez issued a statement criticizing Mr. Biden for policies ''that would advance moral evils'' especially ''in the areas of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , contraception, marriage, and gender.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden and \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  have been political allies for years, especially because of the partnership between the Vatican and the United States during President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's tenure on issues like the normalization of relations with Cuba and the Paris Climate Agreement.\par \par \uc2 Last month \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2 ' top doctrinal official, Cardinal Luis Ladaria, warned the U.S. bishops in a letter that a policy on communion as relates to politicians could ''become a source of discord rather than unity.''\par \par \uc2 The debate will grow in the months ahead, as the doctrine committee moves forward. The document will be one for all Catholics, not individuals, Bishop Rhoades told the bishops.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to accept the church's discipline that those who obstinately persist in manifest grave sin are not to be admitted to holy communion,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 But, he added, ''We haven't even written it yet.''\par \par \uc2 Bishop Kevin C. Rhoades leads the Committee on Doctrine. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MICHELLE DAVIES/THE JOURNAL-GAZETTE, VIA AP) (A1); President Biden and the first lady, Jill Biden, at Mass at St. Matthew's in Washington on Jan. 20, the morning of his inauguration. (PHOTOGRAPH BY DOUG MILLS/THE NEW YORK TIMES); State legislatures have introduced more than 500 \uc2 -restriction measures this year. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SAUL LOEB/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES); The Biden family greeted Pope Francis at Andrews Air Force Base during his visit in 2015. (PHOTOGRAPH BY STEPHEN CROWLEY/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A12) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210619eh6j0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
