\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 President's Silence on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Worries Advocates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer \par \uc2 1,802 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 15\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As a case before the Supreme Court threatens Roe v. Wade and Democrats' urgency grows, many activists believe the president needs to be bolder in defending \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 State legislatures have introduced more than 500 restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past four months, a huge increase from previous years. The Supreme Court plans to take up a case that could weaken or even overturn the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined nearly a half-century ago in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And as \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates sound alarms about what they see as an existential threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, many worry that the leader they helped elect is not meeting the moment.\par \par \uc2 Despite the urgency felt by much of his party, President Biden has said little about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  publicly while in office. In fact, he hasn't said the word itself -- an avoidance so noticeable that one advocacy group has created a website tracking his reluctance, DidBidenSayAbortionYet.org.\par \par \uc2 Many activists fear that Mr. Biden's personal discomfort with the issue is keeping him from leading the Democratic Party into a more offensive position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, both through more aggressive policymaking and leveraging the agenda-setting power of the presidency.\par \par \uc2 ''What we really need is for President Biden to be a bold and transformational leader on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right now, but we haven't seen that yet,'' said \uc2 Gretchen Borchelt\uc2 , vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  and health at the \uc2 National Women's Law Center\uc2 . ''It's a different world from when he was vice president, and so far we haven't felt that recognition of urgency from this administration.''\par \par \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , the White House press secretary, has been just as circumspect in her public appearances. When asked about topics like new state laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the looming court case, she has relied on euphemisms like ''women's fundamental rights'' and ''the right to choose.'' A White House statement in January on the 48th anniversary of the Roe v. Wade decision promised to defend ''\b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 After Mr. Biden signed executive orders expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and overturning restrictions on the use of taxpayer dollars for clinics that refer or counsel patients to terminate pregnancies, he took a victory lap for protecting ''women's health access'' and returning to the policies that existed before former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  took office.\par \par \uc2 ''If you're unable to say the word, you're also going to have trouble making sure that the people who are most impacted get the care and the protections that we need,'' said Renee Bracey Sherman, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocate who started the website monitoring Mr. Biden's reticence. ''To me that's not a champion. That is not someone who is really even trying to show up for people who need abortions.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden's cautiousness on an issue central to his political base comes as he confronts the realities of leading a Democratic Party that shifted sharply to the left during the Trump administration. While Mr. Biden has moved left himself on many issues including \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , liberals are finding their ambitions curtailed on immigration, criminal justice, gun control and other priorities by their razor-thin control of the Senate and a president whom they are pushing to take a more aggressive approach.\par \par \uc2 The disagreement within the party over priorities is particularly acute on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a core part of Democrats' pitch to the suburban and Black women who helped lift Mr. Biden into office. Planned Parenthood and other \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations poured tens of millions of dollars into Mr. Biden's campaign, a recognition of the damage the Trump administration and Republican control of Congress caused to their movement.\par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are particularly challenging personal terrain for Mr. Biden, an observant Catholic who underwent a decades-long conversion to the cause. Some conservative American bishops have called for Mr. Biden, the country's second Catholic president, to be denied communion because of his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a move the Vatican warned against this month.\par \par \uc2 A White House aide on Wednesday declined to comment on the specific criticisms from \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates but said the administration remained committed to protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''The president and the vice president are devoted to ensuring that every American has access to health care, including \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, regardless of their income, ZIP code, race, health insurance status or immigration status,'' Ms. Psaki said at a briefing last week when asked what actions the administration might take if the Supreme Court ruled to undermine Roe.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats pushing legislation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  say they are largely satisfied with the administration's stance.\par \par \uc2 ''They're juggling quite a few policies, but their agenda is an agenda that values \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  for women,'' said Representative Barbara Lee of California, a Democrat who has introduced several bills to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. ''I'm confident that we have a partner in the White House.''\par \par \uc2 No matter what approach Mr. Biden takes to expanding \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, his administration will be starting from behind. Access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more vulnerable than it has been since the constitutional right was won in Roe, in part because of the work of social conservatives who spent years methodically rallying the millions who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from statehouses to Washington.\par \par \uc2 Liberals lost the Supreme Court for a generation when Justice Amy Coney Barrett was confirmed last fall, expanding a conservative majority. If the court moves to overturn Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to quickly become illegal in 22 states. Even if the law is upheld in some form, this year is already on track to be the most damaging state legislative session to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a decade, according to a report by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 After decades in which conventional political wisdom dictated that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  energized the right, many Democratic strategists and candidates now see the issue as a powerful way to mobilize their voters, particularly suburban women. Nationwide, a majority of Americans support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in some or all cases.\par \par \uc2 ''From the presidential race to House races, this is an extremely motivating factor,'' said Meredith Kelly, a Democratic strategist who worked for Senator Kirsten Gillibrand's presidential campaign and for House Democrats' campaign arm during their takeover of the chamber in 2018.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden entered the Senate in 1973 as a 30-year-old, just weeks before the Roe v. Wade decision. He soon concluded that the Supreme Court had gone ''too far'' on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and years later voted for a constitutional amendment that would have allowed states to overturn Roe. He has cast his evolution as a matter of wrestling with the teachings of his faith. But his shifting views over the years also reflect a political calculation about the changing mores of his party.\par \par \uc2 Under pressure from activists and allies early in the 2020 Democratic primary race, Mr. Biden reversed his decades-long support for the Hyde Amendment, a measure that prohibits federal funding for most abortions, and that supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights say all but bans the procedure for poor women and women of color who rely on Medicaid for their health care. Campaign aides who urged Mr. Biden to shift his stance have said his initial reluctance was tied to his faith.\par \par \uc2 Yet as a presidential candidate, Mr. Biden was far less vocal than many of his rivals in the primary, including Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who once compared an Alabama law effectively banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ''a scene from 'The Handmaid's Tale.'''\par \par \uc2 ''If one were to look at him as a Catholic and his attendance at Mass and the way he looks at life and death and everything else, culturally he's like 1,000 percent Catholic,'' said Jo Renee Formicola, a professor of political science at Seton Hall University who studies the relationship between the Catholic Church and American lawmakers. ''He's very, very Catholic, but when it comes to being political, he's much more pragmatic than Catholic.''\par \par \uc2 In office, Mr. Biden has reversed several Trump administration policies, including rolling back restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, removing a ban on federally funded medical research that uses fetal tissue from abortions, and reversing limits on funding for U.S. and international groups that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services or referrals.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates say those early moves fall short. His joint address to Congress did not mention the threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, referring only in passing to ''protecting women's health.'' Ms. Harris, once fairly outspoken on the issue, has made no notable remarks on it since taking office.\par \par \uc2 ''The level of the crisis calls for a stronger level of leadership,'' said Kelley Robinson, the executive director of the Planned Parenthood Action Fund. ''We're looking for them to be explicit champions for sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and to use that bully pulpit to make sure that's a priority that's expressed from the highest office in the land.''\par \par \uc2 Many advocates are looking to the president's fiscal 2022 budget, set to be released on Friday, as a crucial marker of the administration's position. \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations are pushing Mr. Biden to make good on his promise to eliminate the Hyde Amendment and other restrictions on federal money.\par \par \uc2 His administration has also called on Congress to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, which would guarantee \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide even if the Supreme Court overturned Roe. But it has not proposed specific legislation or unveiled any strategy for pushing such a bill through Congress.\par \par \uc2 Many in the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  community believe Mr. Biden must expand his agenda, adopting ideas from the Democratic primary like a federal preclearance requirement for state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws; banning targeted regulations of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers that make it harder for clinics to remain open; making the drugs misoprostol and mifepristone, which induce abortions, available around the country; and nominating only judges who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, an explicit pledge Democrats have long avoided.\par \par \uc2 ''Codifying Roe is basically meaningless in states that have legislated it out of reach. If that's your standard-bearer, then we're having the wrong conversation,'' said Destiny Lopez, a co-president of All* Above All, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group. ''There is an opportunity here to put out a longer-term, broader vision that's based on the reality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access today.''\par \par \uc2 Of the Biden administration, she added: ''They could start by actually saying the word.''\par \par \uc2 A receptionist, above, at the only \uc2  clinic in Mississippi. If the Supreme Court moves to overturn Roe v. Wade, it is likely \uc2  would quickly become illegal in 22 states. President Biden hasn't said the word ''\uc2 '' publicly while in office. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS; STEFANI REYNOLDS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210528eh5s0006a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\