\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What a 'Post-Roe' Nation Might Look Like\b0\par\par\uc2 By Quoctrung Bui, Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,307 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Depending on how the Supreme Court decides a Mississippi \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, access to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be restricted in large parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision Monday to hear a case about a Mississippi law that would ban most abortions after 15 weeks could end up weakening or even overturning Roe v. Wade. Depending on the ruling, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access could effectively end for those living in much of the American South and Midwest, especially those who are poor, according to an analysis updated this week.\par \par \uc2 In more than half of states, though, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access would be unchanged, according to the analysis, a version of which we first covered in 2019. (We have updated our reporting along with the analysis.)\par \par \uc2 ''A post-Roe United States isn't one in which \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't legal at all,'' Caitlin Knowles Myers, an economist at Middlebury College and a co-author of the research, said in our earlier report. She obtained and analyzed the new data for The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  recently. ''It's one in which there's tremendous inequality in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.''\par \par \uc2 Today there is at least one \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in every state, and most women of childbearing age live within an hour's drive or so of one, the analysis found. If Roe were overturned, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be likely to quickly become illegal in 22 states. Forty-one percent of women of childbearing age would see the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic close, and the average distance they would have to travel to reach one would be 279 miles, up from 35 miles now.\par \par \uc2 As distances to clinics increase, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates decline, research shows. Women who can't afford to travel to a legal clinic or arrange child care or leave from work for the trip are most affected. Also, remaining clinics would not necessarily be able to handle increased demand. A study from a different research team on the effects of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic closings in Wisconsin showed a similar relationship between increased drive times and the number of abortions performed at clinics.\par \par \uc2 Without Roe, the number of legal abortions in the United States would be at least 14 percent lower, Professor Myers and her colleagues estimated. That could mean about 100,000 fewer legal abortions a year, they found. The number is impossible to predict precisely, because new clinics could open on state borders, and some people could order \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by mail, or obtain illegal surgical abortions, which may be dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Ordering pills by mail has become easier during the pandemic: The Biden administration has said doctors can use telemedicine to prescribe pills that cause abortions early in pregnancy. The doctors can then mail the pills directly to patients, easing the usual requirement for an in-person visit. That could increase \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in states that continue to allow the procedure, but the policy is tied to the Covid-19 public health emergency, and is not permanent. States that seek to ban or regulate abortions could restrict telemedicine abortions, too, and many already do.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi law that the Supreme Court will consider would ban most abortions after 15 weeks (women usually find out they're pregnant after at least four weeks). That is about two months earlier than Roe and later decisions allow a ban (the exceptions are for a medical emergency or ''severe fetal abnormality''). The justices will consider whether laws restricting abortions earlier than the Roe threshold of fetal viability are unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 The court could decide to reaffirm Roe; or to chip away at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights while upholding Roe; or to overturn it completely. Legal scholars said the appointment of Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who replaced Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  last year, might have changed the calculus of what's possible, as has the court's decision to take this case. The decision is likely to be delivered sometime next year. In her confirmation hearings, Judge Barrett declined to give her views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 If the Mississippi law is upheld, it will allow other states to enact or enforce similar laws. In the last few years, many states have passed laws limiting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas on Wednesday signed a bill that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as early as six weeks into a pregnancy, and allow individuals to sue \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who break the law. Analysts said that it was a new strategy intended to slow down \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers by forcing them to respond to lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 ''I think it's more likely now that we would see them frontally reverse Roe v. Wade than even a year ago,'' said Katherine Franke, director of the center for gender and sexuality law at Columbia. ''But they don't need to. They could certainly accomplish the same end by keeping Roe on paper, but they would have hollowed it out so entirely that it would give a green light to conservative state legislatures to enact laws that essentially overrule Roe.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists and politicians who have sought the elimination of Roe have long pinned their hopes on this strategy, because passing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions through Congress has proved difficult. Nationwide, a majority of Americans support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in some or all cases. But in addition to restrictions based on time limits, as in Mississippi, states have passed laws that require \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to have hospital admitting privileges; require women seeking abortions to wait for long intervals; or restrict the kinds of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures that are allowed.\par \par \uc2 ''It would be a whole lot better for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy if the states were allowed to have their regulations stood up and unchallenged,'' Charmaine Yoest, a former president of \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , said in our earlier report. ''You would have the laws reflecting the folks in those states, and that's what American federalism is supposed to be.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Yoest noted that not every state that would pursue new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations would necessarily ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Long travel distances are already a challenge for women in some areas. In parts of Missouri and Mississippi, where state officials have worked hard to limit abortions, many women live 250 or more miles from the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, far enough that their access wouldn't be changed much if \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were outlawed. In other parts of the country, like the Northeast and the West Coast, where there isn't much support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is also unlikely to change.\par \par \uc2 Ten states have passed so-called trigger laws, which would automatically ban all abortions without Roe. An additional 12 states are considered highly likely to pass new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in a new legal environment, based on recent legislative action and state court rulings. Changes in state politics have made other states, like Wisconsin, less likely to do so.\par \par \uc2 ''In a post-Roe United States, greater power to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is turned to the states,'' Professor Myers said, ''and state politics becomes even more important.''\par \par \uc2 The estimates are based on two elements: research of how recent clinic closings in Texas affected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates among women whose driving distance to providers increased, and two sets of assumptions about which states might outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe were overturned.\par \par \uc2 The research was published in 2019 in the journal Contraception by Professor Myers; Rachel K. Jones, a sociologist at the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a research group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; and Ushma Upadhyay, an associate professor at the University of California, San Francisco. It was updated for The Times last year by Professor Myers to account for changes in state laws and the locations of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics. Since then, despite extensive state legislative activity on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulation, no state has passed a law that would change its category.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210520eh5k00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shp