\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Bill That Essentially Bans  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Is Now Texas Law\b0\par\par\uc2 By Edgar Sandoval and Dave Montgomery \par \uc2 1,240 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott signed one of the nation's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, banning it after six weeks of pregnancy, as Texas lawmakers take a hard-right approach to major issues.\par \par \uc2 SAN ANTONIO -- Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas signed into law on Wednesday one of the nation's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, banning the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy and thrusting the state into the advancing national debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The legislation, also known as the ''heartbeat law,'' amounts to an outright ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as many women are not aware they are pregnant at the six-week mark. It also would allow any private citizen to sue doctors or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic employees who would perform or help arrange for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The Texas law arrives at a potentially pivotal moment in the long fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. This week the Supreme Court announced it would consider a case from Mississippi that could undermine Roe v. Wade, the 1973 decision that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case will be the first on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  under the court's new 6-3 conservative majority, giving anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists hope that their strategy of passing restrictive laws in state legislatures and creating a long pipeline of new cases will pay off. Texas is among at least a dozen states to recently adopt restrictive measures.\par \par \uc2 The bill in Texas also comes two weeks before the end of one of the most staunchly conservative biennial legislative sessions in recent state history. Beyond \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Texas lawmakers have taken a very hard-right approach to a number of major issues and culture war subjects, including voting, gun ownership, policing and L.G.B.T.Q. rights.\par \par \uc2 A combination of soaring growth and diversity in suburbs has changed the once solidly Republican state. Still, Republican victories in last year's election repelled an attempted Democratic ''blue wave'' and further solidified the G.O.P.'s longstanding hold on state government.\par \par \uc2 Flanked by more than three dozen lawmakers and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, Mr. Abbott signed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill on Wednesday, saying: ''Our creator endowed us with the right to life and yet millions of children lose their right to life every year because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Texas, we work to save those lives.''\par \par \uc2 The legislation drew support from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, who broke into applause in Mr. Abbott's office, and condemnation from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists, who are gearing up to challenge it in the courts. According to a recent survey of Texans by the University of Texas and The Texas Tribune, about 40 percent of respondents identified themselves as ''pro-life'' and 41 percent as ''pro-choice.''\par \par \uc2 Joe Pojman, the executive director with the Texas Alliance for Life, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group, said he was confident the state's new law would prevail despite the ''steep hill'' it would face in the courts. He pointed to the growing number of states and localities that have passed restrictive measures.\par \par \uc2 ''People are ready for a change,'' Mr. Pojman said. ''People understand that an unborn child is a baby and society has a responsibility to protect that baby.''\par \par \uc2 State and national Democrats slammed the new law.\par \par \uc2 Christian D. Menefee, the chief civil lawyer for Harris County, the most populous county in Texas, called the new law ''morally reprehensible, unconstitutional, and nothing more than a blatant attempt to limit women's access to health care.''\par \par \uc2 ''I look forward to the courts invalidating this law,'' he said, ''but it's embarrassing that we even got this far.''\par \par \uc2 A number of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state closed after a 2013 law set new standards for Texas facilities that performed abortions, including minimum sizes for rooms and admission privileges at nearby hospitals for doctors. That law was struck down by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  in 2016, and since then, several states have passed a range of measures that have been blocked by federal courts.\par \par \uc2 Texas chapters of Planned Parenthood and \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  recently filed a lawsuit against Lubbock, where voters approved a ''Sanctuary for the Unborn'' ordinance this month. The rule effectively bans all abortions in the city of almost 260,000 residents, where more than two-thirds of voters cast ballots for Donald J. Trump in 2016 and 2020.\par \par \uc2 In a legislative session that has leaned heavily conservative, not all Texans agreed with the priorities set by their legislators. Only 7 percent said the gun debate should be the top priority for lawmakers, and 5 percent said the same for voting rights. Of more concern were the economy, the power grid, the coronavirus pandemic and border security, which ranked highest at 36 percent, according to a recent poll by the Texas Politics Project at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas, long the most populous Republican-dominated state in the country, has increasingly tilted toward a more moderate electorate. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  lost Texas by nearly 16 percentage points in 2012, but \uc2 Joseph R. Biden Jr\uc2 . gained tremendous ground and lost it by just nearly six percentage points in 2020. In the 2018 Senate race, \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  lost to Ted Cruz by just shy of 215,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 But amid such political and demographic changes, Texas Republicans remain galvanized and invigorated in the post-Trump era. Just as Texas relished playing the conservative antidote in the Obama era, the state's Republican leaders are taking on the same role in the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 During one 48-hour period this month, the state's House of Representatives passed several Republican-led measures. With two weeks before the end of the session, legislation passed includes allowing Texans to carry firearms without permits; new voter restrictions mirroring those enacted in Georgia, Florida and other states; penalties against municipal governments attempting to ''defund police''; and proposed restrictions on transgender youth.\par \par \uc2 ''It's certainly been the most conservative session that we've seen in a decade,'' said Brandon Rottinghaus, a professor of political science at the University of Houston. ''The push on guns, on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , on defunding the police have all been national Republican issues. That national rightward trend has hit Texas and it stuck in Texas.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court will not hear arguments in the Mississippi law banning abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy until the fall, and a decision could be more than a year away. Still, advocates for \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they feared that the court, which has a conservative majority, could strike down a 50-year precedent.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , a senior state policy manager at the Guttmacher Institute, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organization, called the Texas \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law ''new and uniquely cruel'' because it would make scores of medical providers vulnerable to lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 ''This ban in Texas is clearly about controlling pregnant people's bodies and preventing them from making decisions about their lives and futures,'' Ms. Nash said. ''It is going to have a chilling effect.''\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval reported from San Antonio, and Dave Montgomery from Austin, Texas. Manny Fernandez contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Edgar Sandoval reported from San Antonio, and Dave Montgomery from Austin, Texas. Manny Fernandez contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Gov. Greg Abbott of Texas last year. While signing an anti-\uc2  law on Wednesday, he said, ''Our creator endowed us with the right to life.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Andrew Caballero-Reynolds/Agence France-Presse -- Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210520eh5k0004m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
