\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Take Case Testing Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,467 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The case, arising from a Mississippi law that bans most abortions after 15 weeks, could undermine the constitutional right established in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- The Supreme Court on Monday plunged back into the contentious debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , agreeing to hear a case that will give its newly expanded conservative majority an opportunity to pare back the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  established nearly a half century ago in Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The new case, concerning a Mississippi law that seeks to ban most abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy -- about two months earlier than Roe and subsequent decisions allow -- is seen by both sides as potentially pivotal in establishing how aggressively the court's new six-justice conservative majority will move to place new constraints on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights denounced the court's decision to take up the case, saying that merely by agreeing to hear it the conservative justices were signaling a willingness to revisit Roe.\par \par \uc2 ''Alarm bells are ringing loudly about the threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said in a statement. ''The Supreme Court just agreed to review an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that unquestionably violates nearly 50 years of Supreme Court precedent and is a test case to overturn Roe v. Wade.''\par \par \uc2 Backers of the Mississippi law said that it was necessary to protect fetal life and expressed confidence that it would be judged constitutional by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 ''The Mississippi Legislature enacted this law consistent with the will of its constituents to promote women's health and preserve the dignity and sanctity of life,'' Lynn Fitch, the state's Republican attorney general, said in a statement. ''I remain committed to advocating for women and defending Mississippi's legal right to protect the unborn.''\par \par \uc2 The justices will hear the case in their next term, starting in October, and are likely to deliver a decision in the spring or early summer next year, as the 2022 midterm elections are gearing up. The stakes of the case ensure that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate will remain a political flash point, rallying conservative and liberals alike.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, the Supreme Court struck down a restrictive Louisiana \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law by a 5-to-4 margin, with Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . providing the decisive vote. His concurring opinion, which expressed respect for precedent but proposed a relatively relaxed standard for evaluating restrictions, signaled an incremental approach to cutting back on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 But that was before Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  died in September. Her replacement by Justice Amy Coney Barrett, a conservative who has spoken out against ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand,'' has changed the dynamic at the court, diminishing the chief justice's power to guide the pace of change.\par \par \uc2 Since the retirement in 2018 of Justice \uc2 Anthony M. Kennedy\uc2 , a cautious supporter of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, state legislatures have enacted scores of restrictions and bans in the hope that personnel changes at the Supreme Court will spur it to reconsider its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 President Donald J. Trump vowed to name justices who would overrule Roe, and three of his appointees now sit on the court. Two of them -- Justices \uc2 Neil M. Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2  -- dissented from the Louisiana decision last year.\par \par \uc2 The justices considered taking the Mississippi case more than a dozen times during their private conferences before deciding to do so, a possible indication of continued sharp divisions among the court's conservatives about how boldly to address the constitutional status of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's next term now includes two blockbusters: the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case and one on whether the Second Amendment protects a constitutional right to carry guns outside the home.\par \par \uc2 ''The Republican-appointed justices appear to be moving even quicker than analysts predicted to make good on their supermajority status,'' Brian Fallon, the executive director of Demand Justice, a progressive advocacy group, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Responding to calls to expand the size of the Supreme Court to counteract recent conservative gains, President Biden has appointed a commission to consider ways to overhaul its structure and processes. Mr. Fallon said the court's docket reflected its lack of concern about the commission's eventual report.\par \par \uc2 ''In opting to hear major cases next term on guns and now \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he said, ''the Roberts court conservatives have issued their verdict on President Biden's commission: They consider it a complete nothing burger.''\par \par \uc2 The new case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, No. 19- 1392, concerns a law enacted in 2018 by the Republican-dominated Mississippi Legislature that banned abortions if ''the probable gestational age of the unborn human'' was determined to be more than 15 weeks. The statute, a calculated challenge to Roe, included narrow exceptions for medical emergencies or ''a severe fetal abnormality.''\par \par \uc2 Lower courts said the law was plainly unconstitutional under Roe, which forbids states from banning abortions before fetal viability -- the point at which fetuses can sustain life outside the womb, or about 23 or 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic sued, saying the law ran afoul of Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the 1992 decision that affirmed Roe's core holding.\par \par \uc2 Judge Carlton W. Reeves of Federal District Court in Jackson, Miss., blocked the law in 2018, saying the legal issue was straightforward and questioning the state lawmakers' motives.\par \par \uc2 ''The state chose to pass a law it knew was unconstitutional to endorse a decades-long campaign, fueled by national interest groups, to ask the Supreme Court to overturn Roe v. Wade,'' Judge Reeves wrote. ''This court follows the commands of the Supreme Court and the dictates of the United States Constitution, rather than the disingenuous calculations of the Mississippi Legislature.''\par \par \uc2 ''With the recent changes in the membership of the Supreme Court, it may be that the state believes divine providence covered the Capitol when it passed this legislation,'' he wrote. ''Time will tell. If overturning Roe is the state's desired result, the state will have to seek that relief from a higher court. For now, the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  has spoken.''\par \par \uc2 A three-judge panel of the United States Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, in New Orleans, affirmed Judge Reeves's ruling. ''In an unbroken line dating to Roe v. Wade, the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases have established (and affirmed, and reaffirmed) a woman's right to choose an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability,'' Judge Patrick E. Higginbotham wrote for the majority.\par \par \uc2 Judge James C. Ho wrote a reluctant concurring opinion expressing misgivings about the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 ''Nothing in the text or original understanding of the Constitution establishes a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he wrote. ''Rather, what distinguishes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from other matters of health care policy in America -- and uniquely removes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy from the democratic process established by our founders -- is Supreme Court precedent.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Fitch, Mississippi's attorney general, urged the justices to hear the state's appeal in order to reconsider their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  jurisprudence. '''Viability' is not an appropriate standard for assessing the constitutionality of a law regulating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 The precise question the justices agreed to decide was ''whether all pre-viability prohibitions on elective abortions are unconstitutional.'' Depending on how the court answers that question, it could reaffirm, revise or do away with the longstanding constitutional framework for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Northup, of the \uc2 Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which represents the clinic challenging the Mississippi law, said the last possibility was alarming.\par \par \uc2 ''The consequences of a Roe reversal would be devastating,'' she said. ''Over 20 states would prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outright. Eleven states -- including Mississippi -- currently have trigger bans on the books, which would instantaneously ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe is overturned.''\par \par \uc2 Lawyers for the clinic said the case was straightforward. The law, they wrote, ''imposes, by definition, an undue burden.''\par \par \uc2 ''It places a complete and insurmountable obstacle in the path of every person seeking a pre-viability \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks who does not fall within its limited exceptions,'' they wrote. ''It is unconstitutional by any measure.''\par \par \uc2 Previous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases have considered restrictions on who can perform the procedure, what kinds of standards clinics must meet and what methods are permissible. The new case will address a far more fundamental issue, said Father Frank Pavone, the national director of Priests for Life.\par \par \uc2 ''This is not a case simply on regulations of the procedure or the places where it is performed,'' he said in a statement. ''This goes right to the matter of prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or, as we would say, protections of the baby.''\par \par \uc2 Demonstrators in Mississippi in 2019. Lower courts have said the law headed to the Supreme Court was unconstitutional under Roe. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANDREA MORALES FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A16) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210518eh5i00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
