\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Opponents Of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Believe Goal Is In Reach\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabrina Tavernise \par \uc2 781 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court on Monday said it would consider a case from Mississippi that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of gestation, a direct challenge to Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists across the country expressed optimism on Monday that they might be on the cusp of achieving a long-held goal of the movement: overturning Roe v. Wade, the 1973 Supreme Court decision that extended federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court announced on Monday morning that it would consider in its next term a case from Mississippi that would ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of gestation, with narrow exceptions. The case is a direct challenge to the 1973 ruling, which prevents states from banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before fetal viability -- around 23 or 24 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 It is the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case under the court's new 6-3 conservative majority, and activists expressed hope that this case would be the one to remove federal protections for the procedure. Such a ruling would give the right to regulate abortions at any point in pregnancy back to the states, many of which in the South and Midwest have imposed tough restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a great sense of inspiration across the country right now,'' said Mike Gonidakis, president of Ohio Right to Life. ''This is the best court we've had in my lifetime, and we hope and pray that this is the case to do it.''\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List, a national anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, called the court's move ''a landmark opportunity to recognize the right of states to protect unborn children,'' and noted that state legislatures have introduced hundreds of bills restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in this legislative season.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates, for their part, said they were shocked that the Supreme Court had taken the case.\par \par \uc2 ''Alarm bells are ringing loudly,'' said \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is representing Jackson Women's Health Organization, the only clinic in Mississippi still performing abortions. ''The consequences of a Roe reversal would be devastating.''\par \par \uc2 In the past, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups often had success in federal appeals courts. But over the years, a concerted effort by Republicans to get more conservatives appointed to the federal bench has shifted the balance. At the very top, the appointment of two conservatives, Amy Coney Barrett and \uc2 Brett M. Kavanaugh\uc2 , to the Supreme Court by former President Donald J. Trump has tipped the balance toward conservatives.\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen how the court will rule on this case. Still, the shifting makeup of the Supreme Court has energized the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement. This year alone, hundreds of bills have passed Republican-controlled state legislatures, largely in the South and the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , which tracks women's \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation, has counted 536 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that passed between January and the end of April, with 61 of those restrictions enacted in 13 states. By this time in 2011, the year ''previously regarded as the most hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights since Roe,'' Guttmacher said, 42 restrictions had been enacted.\par \par \uc2 The group said that Louisiana was the only other state to have passed a 15-week ban. A spokeswoman for Guttmacher said the Louisiana ban would go into effect if the Mississippi law was upheld.\par \par \uc2 For years, Mississippi's Legislature has passed restrictions that have whittled down access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Jackson Women's Health Organization is the only clinic left in the state. The 15-week ban was passed in 2018, and the following year the state, along with a number of others, passed a six-week ban. All of the bans have been suspended while the court system works out whether they will be allowed to stand, given the constraints imposed by Roe and Planned Parenthood v. Casey, the Supreme Court's 1992 decision.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi case will not be decided until 2022, in the Supreme Court's next term. In the meantime, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates said they would continue to work at the state level to restrict access to the procedure. Mr. Gonidakis said his organization was working on getting a so-called trigger ban passed by Ohio legislators, effectively stopping all abortions in the state should Roe v. Wade be overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''You're going to see pro-life legislators across the country rush to pass legislation,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court announced on Monday morning that it would consider a case from Mississippi that would ban \uc2  after 15 weeks of gestation, with narrow exceptions.  (PHOTOGRAPH BY Stefani Reynolds for The New York Times FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210518eh5i0003k\par }\page {\par\fs2