\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 By-Mail  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills Permitted During Pandemic\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 662 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 28\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The agency said it would stop enforcing a rule requiring women to get the first of two pills in person at a medical clinic or hospital.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has decided to allow women to receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by mail for the duration of the coronavirus pandemic, the latest development in an issue that has increasingly taken center stage in the American \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 In a letter sent Monday to two leading organizations representing \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  physicians, the acting commissioner of the Food and Drug Administration said that the agency would temporarily stop enforcing its requirement that the first of two drugs needed to terminate an early pregnancy be dispensed in a medical clinic.\par \par \uc2 The new policy counters a Supreme Court decision in January that sided with the Trump administration, which had appealed a federal judge's decision last July to suspend the requirement. The judge had argued that the requirement put women at risk during the pandemic because they would need to visit clinics in person and often travel significant distances to do so.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through medication, first approved by the F.D.A. in 2000, is increasingly becoming women's preferred method for terminating a pregnancy. As of 2017, research estimated that about 60 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients early enough in pregnancy to be eligible -- 10 weeks pregnant or less -- chose medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  over suction or surgery.\par \par \uc2 But the F.D.A. requires that the first drug in the two-medication regimen, mifepristone, be dispensed in clinics or hospitals by specially certified doctors or other medical providers. For years, \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  experts have urged that the requirement be lifted on the grounds that there are no significant safety reasons for in-person dispensing of a pill that women are then legally allowed to take on their own in any location, and that the restriction places the greatest burden on low-income women and those in areas with limited access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers.\par \par \uc2 For several years, with the F.D.A.'s permission, researchers have been conducting a study that provides telemedicine consultations to women seeking abortions and mails them the pills. Their research has found the approach to be safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 Additional data was collected in recent months from the experiences of women during the pandemic who received \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills by mail after the judge lifted the restriction and before the Supreme Court reinstated it.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Janet Woodcock, the acting F.D.A. commissioner, wrote in her letter to the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  and the \uc2 Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine\uc2  that studies of the pandemic experience ''do not appear to show increases in serious safety concerns,'' like bleeding, ectopic pregnancy or the need for surgical interventions ''occurring with medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a result of modifying the in-person dispensing requirement during the Covid-19 pandemic.''\par \par \uc2 Groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  objected to the decision. Jeanne Mancini, president of March for Life, said in a statement that allowing appointments for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  via telemedicine posed ''grave danger'' to women's safety, adding ''chemical abortions should have more medical oversight not less.''\par \par \uc2 Organizations of medical professionals and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, which sent a letter in March to President Biden and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  asking for the F.D.A. to lift the restrictions during the pandemic, welcomed the decision.\par \par \uc2 ''Mifepristone itself has demonstrated, through both clinical study and decades of use, to be a safe, effective medication,'' the president and chief executive of the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists said in a statement. ''Requiring the medicine to be dispensed in person, then taken elsewhere at the patients' discretion, is arbitrary and does nothing to bolster the safety of an already-safe medicine.''\par \par \uc2 As of 2017, about 60 percent of eligible pregnant women, at 10 weeks or less, chose \uc2  by medication over other methods. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MICHELLE MISHINA-KUNZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210414eh4e0003n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
