\par\par \uc2 Movies, Performing Arts/Weekend Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Devika Girish \par \uc2 350 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 April 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 This hammy period drama tells the story of Dr. Bernard Nathanson, a pioneering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider who later became an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigner.\par \par \uc2 Directed by Nick Loeb and Cathy Allyn, ''Roe v. Wade'' tells the story of Dr. Bernard Nathanson, a pioneering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider from the 1960s who later became an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaigner. Loeb has said that the movie doesn't take sides and tries simply to ''lay out the facts'' surrounding the titular 1973 Supreme Court ruling.\par \par \uc2 But it doesn't take long for the film's agenda to become clear. A confused, sepia-tinted cross between a mafia thriller, a courtroom drama and a saga of prophetic redemption, ''Roe v. Wade'' paints Nathanson and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activist Lawrence Lader (Jamie Kennedy) as the masterminds of a mercenary anti-Catholic conspiracy. They were in cahoots, we're told, with Hollywood, the news media, Protestant clergy and rabbis, with the latter singled out in a caricaturish scene.\par \par \uc2 [Video: Watch on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .]\par \par \uc2 Featuring turns by Stacey Dash, Jon Voight, Tomi Lahren, Milo Yiannopoulos and other prominent conservatives, the film lobs a series of ''gotcha'' moments at the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights movement. These range from references to the documented eugenicist beliefs of Margaret Sanger, the founder of Planned Parenthood, to flimsier claims that Supreme Court justices were unfairly pressured by female relatives to vote in favor of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But the film's coup de gr\u226\'00\'E2ce -- Nathanson's tearful change of heart upon seeing his first sonogram -- dispenses with political arguments for crude sentimentality. Those who disagree that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is akin to murder are unlikely to be persuaded, and even those on the fence might struggle to sit through the hammy acting and poor production values.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade\par \par \uc2 Rated PG-13 for gory descriptions and images of surgical procedures. Running time: 1 hour 52 minutes. Available to rent or buy on Apple TV, Google Play and other streaming platforms and pay TV operators.\par \par \uc2 Nick Loeb in ''Roe v. Wade.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY Quiver Distribution FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210402eh4200047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
