\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden's Top Health Posts Remain Empty as G.O.P. Focuses on Culture Wars\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sheryl Gay Stolberg \par \uc2 1,440 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 March 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Fights over President Biden's nominees to lead the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  have centered on transgender policies, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception coverage.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Nearly two months into the Biden administration, in the thick of the worst public health crisis in a century, the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  remains largely leaderless, with President Biden's nominees to run the sprawling agency under concerted attack from Republicans over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and gender politics.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-three of 31 top leadership posts at the department -- including the secretary of health and human services, the commissioner of the Food and Drug Administration and the assistant secretary for preparedness and response, all critical to fighting the coronavirus pandemic -- are held by career officials in acting capacities.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden has yet to nominate an F.D.A. commissioner or a head of preparedness and response, but other vacancies exist in part because Mr. Biden's top health nominees have been stymied on Capitol Hill, including \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2 , Mr. Biden's nominee for health secretary, and Rachel Levine, the nominee for assistant secretary for health, as Republicans target them in an effort to revive the culture wars.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives and some Catholic leaders have branded Mr. Becerra, the California attorney general, an unqualified ''liberal extremist,'' citing his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. Levine, the first openly transgender person nominated to a position that requires Senate approval, was the subject of a tirade by Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , Republican of Kentucky, who likened sex reassignment surgery to ''genital mutilation'' and demanded to know whether she supports such procedures for minors.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, both Mr. Becerra and Dr. Levine took a step closer toward confirmation. The Senate voted 50 to 49 to break off debate and move to Mr. Becerra's confirmation vote, with a crucial Republican, Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine, siding with Democrats. At the same time, the Senate health committee voted to send Dr. Levine's nomination to the full Senate, with Ms. Collins and another Republican, Senator Lisa Murkowski of Alaska, voting in favor.\par \par \uc2 But even as their nominations have moved forward, conservatives across the country have escalated their attacks against the two and have renewed their push to limit protections for transgender people.\par \par \uc2 Last week, Mississippi's governor, \uc2 Tate Reeves\uc2 , a Republican, signed legislation to ban transgender athletes from competing on girls' or women's sports teams, the first of more than 20 states considering restrictions on athletics or gender-based health care for transgender minors this year.\par \par \uc2 During the Trump administration, social conservatives in posts at the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  spearheaded a governmentwide push to roll back transgender and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, as well as government-mandated access to birth control. Now out of government, some of those officials have joined the attacks on Mr. Biden's nominees.\par \par \uc2 Roger Severino\uc2 , a champion of social conservative causes who ran the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 's civil rights office under Mr. Trump and led the fight against transgender protections, called Dr. Levine a ''divisive pick that goes contrary to President Biden's promise of unity.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats are incredulous.\par \par \uc2 ''We're in the middle of a pandemic,'' complained Senator Chris Coons, Democrat of Delaware and a close ally of Mr. Biden's, adding, ''To have all these other major cabinet positions -- secretary of education, secretary of transportation, secretary of Treasury -- and not have the secretary of H.H.S. strongly suggests to me that there is more politics than legitimate concern about making progress.''\par \par \uc2 A Senate floor vote on Mr. Becerra's confirmation is expected to come on Thursday. If he secured 51 votes, officials expect more jobs to be filled soon.\par \par \uc2 But floor votes have not yet been scheduled for Dr. Levine or for Dr. Vivek Murthy, Mr. Biden's pick for surgeon general, whose nomination was also approved by the Senate health panel on Wednesday. In the meantime, the department is being run by career officials.\par \par \uc2 Former health officials in both Republican and Democratic administrations said that was not ideal.\par \par \uc2 ''Career employees take their cues from the top,'' said Bill Pierce, the department's assistant secretary for public affairs under President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . ''You can move along without that for a while, but you can't do it forever.''\par \par \uc2 Nicole Lurie, the assistant secretary for preparedness and response in the Obama administration, said permanent leadership has become especially important after President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who waged an assault against civil servants, many of whom lost trust that their bosses would back them up -- and have become fearful of making decisions.\par \par \uc2 ''There are a lot of people who might have trusted their instincts before, but that trust has been eroded,'' Dr. Lurie said. ''They need to be sure that they are doing the right thing and that if they step out and do something now that is perceived as risky that nobody is going to cut their neck off for it.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Levine, a pediatrician and former secretary of health in Pennsylvania, has found herself targeted by Mr. Severino, who said he met with Dr. Levine early in his tenure at the department. At the time, he was considering whether to amend an anti-discrimination rule in the Affordable Care Act that the Obama administration had interpreted as protecting transgender people; the Trump administration ultimately cut back those protections.\par \par \uc2 While he said they engaged in a ''very respectful dialogue and a good exchange,'' he challenged Dr. Levine about whether she would favor hormone blockers or sex reassignment surgery for minors -- topics that Senator Paul raised during Dr. Levine's confirmation hearing.\par \par \uc2 ''You're willing to let a minor take things that prevent their puberty, and you think they get that back?'' Senator Paul, who is an ophthalmologist, said angrily at one point. ''You give a woman testosterone enough that she grows a beard -- you think she's going to go back looking like a woman when you stop the testosterone?''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Levine replied, ''Transgender medicine is a very complex and nuanced field with robust research and standards of care.''\par \par \uc2 Detractors have seized on a 2017 speech that Dr. Levine gave describing hormone therapy as a standard of care for transgender youth, and also on a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  post she made in January 2020 about a study showing that transgender youth with access to puberty blocking drugs are at decreased risk of suicide.\par \par \uc2 After the hearing, Senator Chuck Schumer, Democrat of New York and the majority leader, denounced Republicans for ''their attacks on trans people,'' which he called, ''just mean, mean and show a complete lack of understanding, a complete lack of empathy.'' In a statement on Wednesday, Senator Bob Casey, Democrat of Pennsylvania, said Senator Paul's ''questioning was transphobic and a source of pain for many Americans, particularly transgender youth.''\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Severino and his fellow conservatives say Dr. Levine is being evasive. The Conservative Action Project, a coalition of conservative leaders, issued a public letter on Tuesday calling on the Senate to reject Dr. Levine's nomination. It complains that she did not answer Mr. Paul's questions and questions her handling of the coronavirus pandemic, including Pennsylvania's decision to designate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an essential health care service.\par \par \uc2 Opposition to Mr. Becerra centers on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and birth control. Republicans have seized on a suit he brought against the Trump administration to block it from expanding religious exemptions for employers that did not want to provide contraceptive coverage through their insurance plans. The Little Sisters of the Poor, a Catholic organization, later joined the suit.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, led by Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas, have accused Mr. Becerra of suing a group of nuns. ''Becerra did sue nuns. He repeatedly harassed the Little Sisters of the Poor,'' Mr. Cotton wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  last month. ''That's why he should be rejected by the Senate.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Becerra, in his own defense, has said, ''I never sued a nun -- any nuns.'' The fact-checking website PolitiFact rated the statement ''half true,'' noting that the lawsuit did involve an issue on which the Little Sisters had long been a litigant, and that Mr. Becerra continued to press the case after a court allowed them to intervene as a defendant.\par \par \uc2 The pushback against Mr. Becerra appeared to catch the Biden administration off guard. When the Senate Finance Committee took up his nomination, it deadlocked, which forced Mr. Schumer to invoke a special maneuver known as a discharge petition to bring it to the floor for this week's vote.\par \par \uc2 Xavier Becerra \par \par \uc2 