\par\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Foes Losing Patience\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mary Ziegler \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 March 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Taking their cue from Trump, absolutists are going all out to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina last month became the latest state to pass a so-called heartbeat ban, criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after fetal cardiac activity or a heartbeat is detected, typically between six and eight weeks into a pregnancy. A federal court promptly blocked the law from taking effect, underscoring the concerns of some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents that the approach was too extreme and would be counterproductive.\par \par \uc2 Until several years ago, these early \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans struck many, even some red state lawmakers, the same way. But in 2019, state legislatures passed a wave of fetal heartbeat bills. About a dozen states, including South Carolina, have sought to put such a law on their books but have been blocked by the courts. At the moment, the Supreme Court's case law rules out any ban before viability, the point at which survival is possible outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 That 2019 push by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  absolutists, two years into the Trump presidency, was also a push against a more incremental approach that had dominated the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort. The \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , a giant in the movement, had warned the heartbeat campaign would backfire at the Supreme Court. Many conservatives thought it went too far.\par \par \uc2 Not anymore. Because the South Carolina law made exceptions for pregnancies resulting from rape and incest, Republican lawmakers who are relatively moderate voted for the ban. The \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  seems to be turning full circle and now provides information about heartbeat bills.\par \par \uc2 Laws like South Carolina's are the new normal on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and they shed some light on the direction the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is headed in the wake of Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps more than any recent major officeholder, Mr. Trump became synonymous with the fight to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 : He was the first sitting president to attend the March for Life, and he nominated three Supreme Court justices who seemed primed to reverse Roe v. Wade. Some prominent figures in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, including Janet Porter, the architect of the heartbeat campaign, saw Mr. Trump as a savior. With Mr. Trump's rise into the White House, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes were divided over how tightly to embrace the new president. That debate has intensified since his exit.\par \par \uc2 Like the Republican Party, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has its own establishment. Groups like National Right to Life, the \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  List and \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2  are well-oiled machines, with ample budgets, close ties to lawmakers and sophisticated strategies.\par \par \uc2 For decades, leaders of those organizations have played a long game: writing laws that narrow access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in ways they hope will not run afoul of the Supreme Court, then helping red states defend them. While it's true that some Supreme Court justices, including \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , seem ready to overturn Roe tomorrow, even the most experienced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents could not be sure about the rest of the court.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  or Amy Coney Barrett, like Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , worried about damaging the court's institutional legitimacy by reversing Roe too quickly (or without enough of an explanation). Movement lawyers looked for bills that would limit access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without forcing the court's conservative majority out of its comfort zone.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump not only undermined the Republican establishment, he also weakened the conventional anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hierarchy. A more absolutist wing of the movement -- those leading the heartbeat campaign -- made a bid for power. They saw in Mr. Trump the possibility of achieving what everyone in the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement wants: a no-exception, nationwide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The key, as they divined from Mr. Trump's approach to politics, was not courting the majority but energizing the base.\par \par \uc2 With an increasingly polarized electorate and gerrymandered districts, red state lawmakers were happy to indulge anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  absolutists, regardless of whether a majority of the state's voters agreed.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina offers a look at where anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement is headed. Until now, those seeking to deal Roe a death by a thousand cuts championed incremental limits on the procedure, while absolutists refused to compromise. Now, it seems the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  establishment has lost control.\par \par \uc2 Polls suggest that Americans may not be comfortable with abortions after the first trimester. Most support some restrictions on the procedure, yet large majorities oppose criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially early in pregnancy. Like their allies in the South Carolina legislature, however, many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders don't seem to care. What counts as compromise to them is an exception for rape and incest that requires \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers to give a victim's contact information to a sheriff within 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 That's because the era of tailoring strategies to public opinion -- or to the most likely response of the Supreme Court -- may be coming to an end. There may still be an establishment wing of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement in the post-Trump era, but what passes for a moderate approach has changed irrevocably.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler is a professor of law at Florida State University and the author of ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Law in America: Roe v. Wade to the Present.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY Will Oliver/EPA, via Shutterstock FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210304eh340004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
