\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Lack of Birth Control Deepens Women's Burden in Venezuela\b0\par\par\uc2 By Julie Turkewitz, Isayen Herrera and Meridith Kohut \par \uc2 2,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 February 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Venezuelan Women Lose Access to Contraception, and Control of Their Lives\par \par \uc2 Leer en espa\u241\'00\'F1ol\par \par \uc2 SAN DIEGO DE LOS ALTOS, Venezuela -- The moment Johanna Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n, 25, discovered she was going to have her sixth child she began to sob, crushed by the idea of bringing another life into a nation in such decay.\par \par \uc2 For years, as Venezuela spiraled deeper into an economic crisis, she and her husband had scoured clinics and pharmacies for any kind of birth control, usually in vain. They had a third child. A fourth. A fifth.\par \par \uc2 Already, Ms. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n was cooking meager dinners over a wood fire, washing clothing without soap, teaching lessons without paper. Already, she was stalked by a fear that she could not feed them all.\par \par \uc2 And now, another child?\par \par \uc2 ''I felt like I was drowning,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 As Venezuela enters its eighth year of economic crisis, a deeply personal drama is playing out inside the home: Millions of women are no longer able to find or afford birth control, pushing many into unplanned pregnancies at a time when they can barely feed the children they already have.\par \par \uc2 Around Caracas, the capital, a pack of three condoms costs $4.40 -- three times Venezuela's monthly minimum wage of $1.50.\par \par \uc2 Birth control pills cost more than twice as much, roughly $11 a month, while an IUD, or intrauterine device, can cost more than $40 -- more than 25 times the minimum wage. And that does not include a doctor's fee to have the device put in.\par \par \uc2 With the cost of contraception so far out of reach, women are increasingly resorting to abortions, which are illegal and, in the worst cases, can cost them their lives.\par \par \uc2 The situation is a major departure from what Venezuela's government once promised its women and girls. \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2 , the father of the country's socialist-inspired revolution, declared that his government would grant women what others had not: full and equal participation in society.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez brought women into the halls of power and enshrined in the Constitution the right to ''decide freely'' how many children a couple wished to have. In a region where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is largely banned, he stopped short of legalizing the procedure. But birth control was subsidized and widely available.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez and his successor, President Nicol\u225\'00\'E1s Maduro, publicly declared themselves to be feminists.\par \par \uc2 But as Mr. Maduro's grip on the country has hardened into authoritarian rule, Venezuela's economy has collapsed under the weight of corruption, mismanagement and American sanctions.\par \par \uc2 The nation that was once Latin America's wealthiest is mired in a crisis economists have called the world's worst in decades, outside of war, with its population suffering from runaway inflation and widespread hunger.\par \par \uc2 And Venezuelans now face a health system so broken that it can no longer reliably provide basic contraception. Today, amid the collapse of the country's public health system, birth control is nearly absent from government clinics and available at private pharmacies only at prohibitive prices.\par \par \uc2 The result has been life-changing for women, who shoulder the vast majority of child care responsibilities, just as the crisis has greatly expanded the challenge of being a parent.\par \par \uc2 Many women who grew up believing that Mr. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez's political movement, known as Chavismo, would springboard them out of poverty, offering them education and career opportunities, now face the task of raising four, six or 10 children at a time when the basics of family care -- food, soap, diapers -- arrive intermittently or not at all.\par \par \uc2 Anitza Freitez, a demographer with the Universidad Cat\u243\'00\'F3lica Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Bello in Caracas, said this dynamic could shape the country for decades, creating ''a vicious circle of poverty.''\par \par \uc2 As Venezuela's maternity wards fell apart, maternal deaths surged 65 percent between 2015 and 2016, according to the country's health ministry.\par \par \uc2 And then the government stopped releasing data.\par \par \uc2 Fexsibel Bracho was 24 and the mother of three when she sought out a clandestine clinic to end a pregnancy in January. The procedure, performed with a hook, punctured her uterus. She died of a hemorrhage on Feb. 2.\par \par \uc2 ''She didn't have money for birth control,'' said her mother, Lucibel Marcano, 51, who cared for Fexsibel in her final hours, watching her daughter's face lose color.\par \par \uc2 Representatives of the country's health and women's ministries did not respond to letters or emails requesting interviews.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Hugo Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez\uc2  was elected president in 1998, he inherited a system in which birth control was already widely available.\par \par \uc2 In his push to embrace women's rights, he offered direct cash transfers to poor mothers and moved the women's ministry into the executive branch.\par \par \uc2 ''Without true liberation of women, full liberation of the people will be impossible,'' Mr. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez said in 2009, ''and I am convinced that a true socialist must also be a true feminist.''\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  was never a priority, something even his supporters recognize.\par \par \uc2 Chavismo, said Indhira Libertad Rodr\u237\'00\'EDguez, 45, a women's rights activist and longtime supporter of Mr. Ch\u225\'00\'E1vez, ''has not overcome a bias against women as reproducers.''\par \par \uc2 As Venezuela's economy -- long buoyed by its vast oil reserves -- began to tumble in 2014, the result of plummeting crude oil prices and poor financial management, the government's purchasing power dove.\par \par \uc2 By 2015, contraceptives, once free at government hospitals and broadly affordable at private pharmacies, began to disappear. And women who could once plan their futures -- thanks to contraception -- began to lose control.\par \par \uc2 By 2018, oral contraceptives, implants and patches were nearly impossible to find in several major cities, according to a study by the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  coalition Equivalencies in Action.\par \par \uc2 Some couples began to ration or abstain from sex. Others tried to plan around a woman's menstrual cycle. But it didn't always work. And not everyone has a choice.\par \par \uc2 As the crisis has sharpened, many women say that abuse has, too, making it difficult for them to say no to a partner or to leave a relationship.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n delivered her sixth child, Yorkeinys, in April, when the country was in the grip of the pandemic and her husband, a mechanic, had been out of work for weeks. When she arrived home from the hospital, there were only lentils in the pantry, she said, and all of her children were hungry. She lapsed into a depression and spent 20 days in bed.\par \par \uc2 ''Like a pit with no exit,'' is how she described her worst days. ''All dark, you turn here, and it's all dark, you turn there, and it's all dark.''\par \par \uc2 The plan she'd nurtured since she was a child -- to become a chemist -- is indefinitely on hold.\par \par \uc2 As raising children in Venezuela has become increasingly difficult, the number of women seeking abortions has surged, according to interviews with health professionals and community workers across the country.\par \par \uc2 Before the economic crisis, some doctors would perform abortions illegally in safer facilities. But about half of the country's physicians, some 30,000 people, have left in recent years, according to the Venezuelan Medical Federation, driving women to makeshift clinics.\par \par \uc2 In the shadows, some women, and a few men, have become part of an expanding group of underground \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counselors, mostly trying to educate women in how to find and use misoprostol, a drug used legally in other countries to induce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The idea is to keep women away from shady \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  purveyors who charge high prices for possibly deadly operations.\par \par \uc2 Faldas-R, an activist group that runs an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  support hotline, reported a 40 percent rise in calls between 2018 and 2019.\par \par \uc2 Women arrested for ending their pregnancies, and the practitioners who help them, can spend years in prison.\par \par \uc2 At great personal risk, a few counselors meet women to deliver misoprostol.\par \par \uc2 But even with guidance, the experience can be excruciating, often involving a frantic search for the $150 it costs to buy the pills, followed by a hunt for a safe place to hide away to bleed for a few hours.\par \par \uc2 One night in late 2019, Jessika, 21, a university student, had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an auto parts warehouse, accompanied by two friends.\par \par \uc2 Jessika had never been able to afford birth control. She said she got pregnant after an assault by her boyfriend, and knew she could not support a child.\par \par \uc2 ''In the country we live in,'' she said, ''a woman is not afforded the luxury of one more mouth to feed.''\par \par \uc2 Through her contacts, she reached one of the counselors, who gave her instructions and wished her luck.\par \par \uc2 Seven weeks pregnant, she bought misoprostol online, from a man who called himself ''Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Vende Todo,'' or ''Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Who Sells Everything.''\par \par \uc2 She knew her mother would not approve, and that she could not have the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at home. So she went to the warehouse, on loan from a friend, holing herself up in a white-walled office with a couch and a single window, keeping it closed so no one would hear her scream.\par \par \uc2 She took the first two pills at 7 p.m., and the second dose two hours later. Soon, she doubled over in pain and began to bleed profusely. Her legs shook, she cried out, and then she fainted.\par \par \uc2 Not all abortions with misoprostol are this painful or risky. Doctors recommend that women take misoprostol with another drug, mifepristone, which prepares the body for the process, making the procedure easier.\par \par \uc2 But mifepristone is difficult to find in Venezuela, and so most women do it the hard way.\par \par \uc2 When Jessika came to, her friends urged her to go to the hospital.\par \par \uc2 ''Don't take me anywhere,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 She was terrified of the police.\par \par \uc2 Afterward, she spent weeks replaying the events of the night.\par \par \uc2 ''You say to yourself, 'Well, this happened, but it could have been worse. It could have gone differently. I could have died in the process, but I didn't, and it's OK,''' she said.\par \par \uc2 ''But it's not OK,'' she went on.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not OK that I had to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a warehouse. It's not OK that I passed out, that I became depressed, it's not OK that I feel the way I do,'' she said, words tumbling out in anger. ''It's not OK that the country pushes you into this desperation, that all it does it close its doors to you. I am resilient, yes. But at some point, all of us get tired. And I am tired. I am so tired.''\par \par \uc2 In the absence of other help, a few nonprofit organizations have become critical resources for women, offering low-cost or free contraception. Most are supported by international funds.\par \par \uc2 At the five clinics run by one of those organizations, Plafam, the waiting rooms are always packed. Women sometimes sleep outside, desperate to be among those who receive free birth control implants on distribution days.\par \par \uc2 Fexsibel Bracho, the young woman who died after a botched \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , never reached Plafam, or one of the counselors.\par \par \uc2 Instead, she went to the makeshift clinic alone, without sharing her plan with her mother or sister.\par \par \uc2 Afterward, her mother struggled to understand her daughter's decision.\par \par \uc2 ''If I could turn back time,'' she said, ''I would talk to her, and tell her not to.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Bracho's sister, Fanix Bracho, 34, said she understood the decision perfectly.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very difficult to be a woman in Venezuela,'' she said. ''I would have done the same.''\par \par \uc2 Johanna Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n with two of her six children in northern Vene- zuela, where contraceptives are difficult to find, let alone afford. (A1); Johanna Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n at home with her children in San Diego de los Altos, in the mountains near Caracas. When she learned she was having a sixth child, Ms. Guzm\u225\'00\'E1n said, ''I felt like I was drowning.''; Left, Maria Ferreira, 23, and her husband, Joseph Cordova, 25, carefully plan their sex life around the number of con- doms they can afford each month. Right, Pilar, a mother of two children, enduring a drug-induced \uc2  at home.; Jessika, 21, who had a painful \uc2  alone in 2019. A sign behind her reads ''Mother by choice, not by punishment.'' (PHOTOGRAPHS BY MERIDITH KOHUT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A12) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF0000202