\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Erratic Policy on Medical Care for Women Is Early Diplomacy Test for Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lara Jakes \par \uc2 1,334 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. funding for overseas health providers that offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services has vacillated with the changing of administrations for decades. Congress is debating whether to settle the policy by law.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- As it reaches out to allies rattled by four years of erratic American diplomacy, the Biden administration wants to enlist Congress in advancing foreign policies that will withstand the whims of any single president.\par \par \uc2 An early test lies in the fate of what is known as the Mexico City policy.\par \par \uc2 Enacted in 1984 by \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , the policy bans U.S. taxpayer funds for health providers overseas that, as part of their medical care for women, offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. It has been rescinded by every Democratic president since, only to be put back into place by every Republican.\par \par \uc2 Even when the ban is rescinded -- as President Biden did on Jan. 28 -- the money cannot be used to pay for abortions.\par \par \uc2 Instead, and by law, it is used to pay for other health care for women and girls in some of the world's poorest nations, allowing providers to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs with other resources. But when the ban is restored, humanitarian groups that are backed by the United Nations and \uc2 U.S. Agency for International Development\uc2  must scramble for other support to cover health care costs.\par \par \uc2 The constant churn has thrown medical budgets into disarray and forced the shuttering of some clinics.\par \par \uc2 Crucially, it also has reinforced the notion among some foreign allies of America as an unreliable partner on global needs.\par \par \uc2 Charles Michel\uc2 , the European Council president, said on Wednesday that he hoped the Biden administration will work more closely with international partners on a range of issues, in contrast with what he described as a ''difference with the last years.''\par \par \uc2 ''We want more fairness, more predictability; we want more stability,'' Mr. Michel told an \uc2 Atlantic Council\uc2  forum.\par \par \uc2 Under President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , the Mexico City policy was not only enforced but expanded, denying aid to additional groups that also provide H.I.V. medicine and tuberculosis treatment. That served as further injury to the insult of American retrenchment from global alliances under Mr. Trump's watch, on everything from military conflicts to diplomatic accords, which the Biden administration now wants to reverse.\par \par \uc2 Last month, on his first full day in office, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony J. Blinken\uc2  said the Biden administration would turn to Congress to help cement a host of major foreign policy decisions to provide continuity from president to president.\par \par \uc2 ''It is hard to have a sustainable foreign policy absent the informed consent of the American people,'' Mr. Blinken told reporters on Jan. 27. ''The place that, in our system, that informed consent is vitally important, is with Congress.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden lifted the Mexico City policy a day later, releasing access to hundreds of millions of dollars in aid to health clinics that had been denied the funding, and pleasing diplomats around the world.\par \par \uc2 ''Norway is looking forward to work closely with the USA again in the global effort'' to protect the \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  of women and girls, Foreign Minister Ine Eriksen Soreide said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Looking forward to strengthening ties with Biden/Harris administration on sexual and \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rights,'' echoed Flemming Moller Mortensen, Denmark's minister for development cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Experts said members of Congress who oppose the Mexico City policy -- mostly Democrats and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists -- have a narrow but rare opportunity to abolish it.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic House previously approved a measure to block the policy, also known by critics as the global gag rule, but it was stalled under Republicans in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 This month, Democrats reclaimed control of the Senate for the first time since 2015 by the slimmest of margins, with a 50-to-50 split and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  able to break tie votes.\par \par \uc2 However, at least two Republicans, Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and Lisa Murkowski of Alaska, also support legislation to permanently end the Mexico City policy, meaning ''there might be a newer window to make something happen,'' said Jen Kates, the director of global health and H.I.V. policy at the \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I think everyone will agree, whatever side they're on, on the issue, is that the Ping-Ponging is a very challenging way to have this policy operate,'' Ms. Kates said, discussing the work of nongovernmental organizations. ''It provides no stability or predictability for N.G.O.s, and it's disruptive.''\par \par \uc2 Opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights also want to see the matter become settled law, but in a way that enshrines the Mexico City policy to immediately prevent Mr. Biden and future presidents from supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers abroad.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , Republican of Utah, revived a plan he had introduced in previous congressional terms to make the funding ban permanent. In a Feb. 3 speech on the Senate floor, he said it was necessary to prevent the policy from being ''rescinded and reinstated again and again between changing administrations.''\par \par \uc2 ''The lives of babies and the dignity of women are not political footballs,'' Mr. Lee said. ''Women and unborn children everywhere have immeasurable dignity and worth, regardless of where they are from. And they ought to be entitled to the right to life and protection from harm -- regardless of who is in office.''\par \par \uc2 His remarks drew an exasperated response from Senator Jeanne Shaheen, Democrat of New Hampshire, who reminded officials that overseas health providers can use U.S. funds only for other medical needs -- not to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, including counseling.\par \par \uc2 So far, she said, 47 senators have committed to a proposal that would rescind the funding ban for good. Other Democrats are expected to join the effort.\par \par \uc2 ''We need a renewed commitment to comprehensive family planning,'' Ms. Shaheen said, one that ''would ensure that care is not limited, based on the president in the White House.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kates noted that whatever Congress approves as law when it is controlled by Democrats could, in theory, be reversed when Republicans again take over. ''This is contested space,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 But she also predicted that Ms. Shaheen's legislation, if enacted, would have ''more of a protective frame around it'' because it would change a 60-year old law that governs how the United States provides foreign assistance, as opposed to amending a spending bill that is debated every year.\par \par \uc2 No other country in the world withholds health care support from United Nations programs over opposition to abortions.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, before Mr. Trump cut the funds, the United States provided $69 million to the \uc2 U.N. Population Fund\uc2 , the world body's leading provider of family-planning services. With the money restored, the agency projects it could provide family planning and health care to 4.2 million women and girls within one year, potentially preventing 1.4 million unintended pregnancies and 640,000 transmissions of sexually transmitted diseases.\par \par \uc2 Natalia Kanem\uc2 , the U.N. agency's executive director, described a mix of joy and relief among member nations upon hearing this month that the United States would restore funding to foreign providers. In an interview, she was careful not to discuss the internal American political debate over whether Congress should put the seesawing U.S. policy to rest.\par \par \uc2 But she said the coronavirus pandemic and poverty and strife in some of the world's most unstable countries underscored that ''we don't just need to get back to where we were four years ago, or 20 years ago.''\par \par \uc2 Given the unpredictability of international support that has led to dwindling health services for women and girls, American officials should pursue ''something with ambition, rather than just tinkering around the edges,'' Dr. Kanem said.\par \par \uc2 ''Funding stable partnerships saves lives,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Training for a hotline in Pakistan. President Biden has released millions of dollars in aid to clinics. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ABDUL MAJEED/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210212eh2c0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
