\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Protesters in Poland Vow to Fight  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 By Monika Pronczuk, Isabella Kwai and Anatol Magdziarz \par \uc2 1,113 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women's rights advocates and thousands of their allies vowed to continue fighting a near-total ban on abortions that came into effect on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Women's rights advocates and allies in Poland vowed on Thursday to continue to fight a near-total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling it a breach of human rights and a sign that the country is regressing.\par \par \uc2 The constitutional court ruling, which abruptly came into effect Wednesday night, tightened Poland's already restrictive laws to further ban abortions in cases of fetal abnormalities. It immediately spurred thousands of outraged Poles to take to the streets to express their defiance, despite limits on public gatherings because of the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ''This decision is a declaration of war,'' Marta Lempart, a protest organizer, said in an phone interview on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 When the ruling was first announced in October, it set off a month of protests on a scale not seen since the 1989 collapse of communism.\par \par \uc2 It was unclear precisely why the government, after delaying implementation of the ruling for months in the face of the protests, moved suddenly to bring it into legal force on Wednesday. The move came as Poland is struggling through the economic repercussions of the pandemic, a partial lockdown and a sluggish vaccine rollout.\par \par \uc2 The action could pose political risks for the governing Law and Justice Party, judging by polls that have shown an overwhelming majority oppose the ban.\par \par \uc2 More protests are planned this week, and on Thursday night hundreds of people turned out in Warsaw under a heavy police presence. Among the protesters was Nadia Klos, a member of the Queen Tour, an L.G.B.T. group.\par \par \uc2 ''The way they are forcing through changes in the midst of the pandemic is unbelievable,'' Ms. Klos said. ''It's an attempt to take away the rights of half of the citizens by referring to religion, when it's all about power.''\par \par \uc2 Another protester, Iwonna Kowalska, with a group called the Polish Grandmas, said the ruling was ''a step backward.''\par \par \uc2 ''This is what the communists would do, too,'' Ms. Kowalska said. ''They would wait for a time when everything is collapsing and then make changes.''\par \par \uc2 The demonstration grew tense early Friday, when police officers told 100 or more protesters they had to show identification before they could leave the area, video from the scene showed. Officers appeared to block the protesters, television coverage showed, dragging many away. Local news outlets reported a number of arrests, although the precise number was not immediately clear.\par \par \uc2 The Constitutional Tribunal, the country's top court, which issued the ruling, explained its decision by saying that ''human life has value in every phase of its evolution, and as a value, the source of which is in the constitutional laws, it should be protected by lawmakers.''\par \par \uc2 Far-right lawmakers and supporters of the ban welcomed legal enforcement of the ruling.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from fetal abnormalities should be prohibited, said Beata Kempa, a Polish member of the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2 , adding that she had been upset by discussions in the \uc2 European Parliament\uc2  on the issue. ''Nobody at all mentioned the right of a child to live.''\par \par \uc2 Anita Czerwinska, a spokeswoman for the Law and Justice party, described the protests as ''a cynical battle against the government.''\par \par \uc2 Even before the tribunal's decision, Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were among the most restrictive in Europe, allowing for termination of pregnancies only in cases of rape or incest, a threat to a woman's life and fetal abnormalities. In practice, most legal abortions -- 1,074 of 1,100 performed in 2019 -- resulted from fetal abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 The right-wing Law and Justice party tried to implement a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in 2016 and 2018, but backed off after mass demonstrations. This time, the government introduced the ban by using the tribunal, which it effectively took over in 2016 as part of a judiciary overhaul that has been criticized at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 The decision by the tribunal cannot be appealed.\par \par \uc2 ''The only possible action is on the international level, through the \uc2 European Court of Human Rights\uc2  and the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . committees,'' said Adam Bodnar, the country's human rights officer. ''On the national level, the only way this decision could be reversed is by changing the government.''\par \par \uc2 The next elections are scheduled in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The ruling has a very personal dimension for millions of Polish women.\par \par \uc2 ''Women are really scared to get pregnant right now,'' said Dominika Sitnicka, a journalist at OKO.press, a news outlet that conducts research and analysis. ''Yesterday was not just a symbol of something. It was doomsday.''\par \par \uc2 As a result of the ban, Polish women will be forced to ''travel abroad or have clandestine abortions -- for those who can afford it,'' said Dunja Mijatovic, the human rights commissioner for the Council of Europe.\par \par \uc2 Though doctors in Poland who perform the procedure in cases of fetal abnormalities now face up to three years in prison, advocates are calling on them to defy the ban.\par \par \uc2 ''Everyone has a choice,'' said Ms. Lempart, one of the protest organizers. ''Will you be an officer of the system, or will you be an honest citizen? We are speaking to every single doctor who will defy this decision: We are with you, and we will help you.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has long been a contentious issue in Poland, a staunchly Roman Catholic country, and the current debate has underlined a societal divide between traditional religious values and more secular ones.\par \par \uc2 The Polish word for ''apostasy,'' or the official procedure of leaving the church, has been trending on \uc2 Google\uc2 , while the level of support for the institution among young Poles has reached a historical low.\par \par \uc2 The government has tried to frame the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate as an attack on the church and therefore an attack on the people, said Edit Zgut, a fellow at the \uc2 German Marshall Fund of the United States\uc2 , a move that could further polarize an already divided society.\par \par \uc2 Many Polish women say they are tired of being used as pawns in the culture wars.\par \par \uc2 Julka Tomiczek came out to protest in Warsaw on Thursday with her daughter and her partner. She wore a black face mask with a red thunderbolt, a symbol for some.\par \par \uc2 ''What brought me here is revolt, dissension, rage,'' she said. ''I don't agree to move back to the Middle Ages. I don't agree to sadistic rulings.''\par \par \uc2 Thousands of outraged Poles have taken to the streets to protest a court ruling tightening Poland's already restrictive \uc2  laws. (PHOTOGRAPH BY WOJTEK RADWANSKI/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210129eh1t0004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
