\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECT\par \b \uc2 Thailand Legalizes Early-Term Abortions but Keeps Other Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Muktita Suhartono and Mike Ives \par \uc2 642 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 January 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 The New York Times on the Web\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Women who end a pregnancy after 12 weeks could still face prison or fines. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates claimed a partial victory.\par \par \uc2 BANGKOK -- In what \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates called a partial victory, Thailand's Parliament has voted to make the procedure legal in the first trimester, while keeping penalties in place for women who undergo it later in their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers in the Senate voted 166 to 7 on Monday to amend a law that had imposed prison terms of up to three years for anyone having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and up to five years for those who perform one. The new version allows any woman to end a pregnancy in the first 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Advocates say the measure is a good start but doesn't go far enough: Anyone in Thailand who seeks an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 12 weeks, except under conditions set by the country's Medical Council, still faces potential fines and up to six months in prison.\par \par \uc2 The Medical Council says pregnancies can be terminated by a qualified professional after 12 weeks if they are the result of a sexual assault or pose a threat to the mother's physical or emotional health. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also permitted if the fetus is known to have abnormalities.\par \par \uc2 Many women in Thailand found ways to get abortions under the previous restrictions, but the country still has a high teenage pregnancy rate. About 1.5 million babies were born to teenage mothers in Thailand between 2000 and 2014, according to government figures analyzed by the \uc2 United Nations Population Fund\uc2 , and nearly 14 percent of all pregnancies in 2016 were among adolescents.\par \par \uc2 Supecha Baotip, an activist with Tamtang, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocacy group in Thailand, said she feared that underground abortions would continue. ''I don't want women with pregnancies older than 12 weeks worried that they cannot undergo the procedure and thus not seek it out legally,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Supecha said she would watch closely to see whether the Health Ministry extends early \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and pressures doctors to comply with the new rule.\par \par \uc2 ''Any hospital can provide this service, but because of the doctors' attitude, they don't,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 Last February, Thailand's Constitutional Court ruled that the country's previous law on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unconstitutional, and it gave the government 360 days to change it.\par \par \uc2 Two revisions were proposed, one by the cabinet and another by the opposition Move Forward Party. The House of Representatives later rejected the Move Forward version, which would have allowed abortions until 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Some elements of Thailand's Buddhist-dominated culture are socially conservative. Yet Thailand also has relatively progressive policies on gender and L.G.B.T.Q. issues.\par \par \uc2 Heather Barr, the interim co-director of the Women's Rights Division at \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 , wrote this week that Thailand's new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law was a step forward but that the late-term restrictions still posed health risks. ''When governments restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , women still have abortions -- they just have more dangerous ones,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Barr said in an email on Thursday that Southeast Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws were still a patchwork, ranging from complete bans to full decriminalization. But she said she saw progress both in Thailand and in South Korea, where a court ruled two years ago that an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law was unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 ''We hope that these court cases will help other governments in the region, and beyond, to realize that restrictions on choice violate international human rights law, and often domestic law as well, and must be reformed,'' Ms. Barr said.\par \par \uc2 Muktita Suhartono reported from Bangkok, and Mike Ives from Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Supporting \uc2  rights at a march in Bangkok in November. (PHOTOGRAPH BY Lauren Decicca/Getty Images FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210128eh1s0005i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
