\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court After Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Now that his presidency has disintegrated into mayhem, how are the conservative justices processing his tenure?\par \par \uc2 I've been wondering what Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  thinks about his former law clerk, Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I don't mean to sound snarky, and I'm certainly not implying that the chief justice bears the slightest responsibility for his law clerk's subsequent effort to overthrow a democratic election.\par \par \uc2 Rather, I mean to open the door to a deeper inquiry: Now that the Trump presidency has disintegrated into mayhem and madness, how are the conservative members of the Supreme Court processing these past four years?\par \par \uc2 The court has been \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's enabler to an unfortunate degree, from upholding his Muslim ban, in one of the sorriest excuses for a Supreme Court decision in recent years, to permitting him to spend money that Congress had not appropriated in order to build his border wall in a location Congress had forbidden. And let's not forget last summer's decision upholding the administration's policy to permit employers with a religious or even just a ''moral'' objection to contraception to withhold from female employees an insurance benefit to which the Affordable Care Act entitles them.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the administration didn't win every Supreme Court battle it chose to fight. Thanks to Chief Justice Roberts and his liberal colleagues, who numbered four before the death of Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  in September, President Trump was unable to add a citizenship question to the 2020 census and he didn't get away with stripping the ''Dreamers'' of their protection against deportation.\par \par \uc2 But the court's basic posture has been one of deference. On Tuesday night, as the House of Representatives was debating a request to Vice President Mike Pence to invoke the 25th Amendment, the justices voted 6 to 3 to grant a bold administration request to skip the intermediate appeals court and lift a district court order that enabled women who want the medication that causes an early \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to get the pill without an in-person visit to a medical office.\par \par \uc2 Early in the coronavirus pandemic, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  had removed the in-person requirement for other drugs, but refused to do so for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug, mifepristone. That makes no sense, given the safety of mifepristone compared with, for example, opioids, which received the F.D.A. pandemic exception. That left the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug as the only one with an in-person requirement at a time when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are ever fewer and ever harder to reach in many parts of the country, a situation that plainly suits the Trump administration and a majority of the Supreme Court just fine. The court voted to lift the district court's injunction while the case continues on appeal. Presumably, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will make short work of the Trump policy and render the whole case moot. The afternoon of Jan. 20 would be a good target date.\par \par \uc2 Maybe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is simply different in the mind of the court's majority. Maybe those six justices were unable or unwilling to grasp the irrefutable arguments Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  raised in dissent. It was pointless, she observed, to require women to pick up the medication in person ''even though physicians may provide all counseling virtually, women may ingest the drug unsupervised at home, and any complications will occur long after the patient has left the clinic.'' (Justice Elena Kagan signed Justice Sotomayor's dissent, while Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  simply noted his own dissent.)\par \par \uc2 The answer matters because after next week, it will be the Biden administration seeking the court's attention. Will the justices be more skeptical, less deferential, more or less willing to depart from normal procedures when the president comes knocking on the door?\par \par \uc2 The answer also matters on a deeper institutional level. Some justices have drifted quite far from their ideological starting points. Harry Blackmun, John Paul Stevens and David Souter come to mind. All were Republican-appointed justices (by Presidents Richard Nixon, Gerald Ford and George H.W. Bush, respectively) who ended their careers as among the most liberal members of the court they served on. Could history repeat itself with any members of the Roberts court?\par \par \uc2 For two of the three relatively young conservatives President Trump has named to the court, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , it's unlikely. There has been a fair amount of academic attention to the question of justices' ''ideological drift.'' One of the more interesting conclusions was drawn by Michael Dorf, a professor at Cornell Law School, in a 2007 article, ''Does Executive Branch Experience Explain Why Some Republican Supreme Court Justices 'Evolve' and Others Don't?''\par \par \uc2 Examining Republican-appointed justices since the mid-20th century (these were most of the justices, since Democratic presidents have had bad luck in getting Supreme Court vacancies), the ones least likely to change their ideological stripes were those with substantial previous experience in the federal executive branch. Justices Gorsuch and Kavanaugh fit that description, as do Justices \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and Chief Justice Roberts. Earlier, Chief Justice William Rehnquist and Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  fit the pattern. Exploring several theories, Professor Dorf concluded that executive branch experience provided ''an especially reliable predictor'' of whether a justice would retain a conservative voting pattern.\par \par \uc2 The observation makes sense; as Professor Dorf noted, a nominee who has been an inside player is likely to have left more clues about his views and is more easily vetted than someone who made a career on a distant court in a region with different politics. By contrast, the experience of arriving in Washington, D.C., in midlife, under a bright spotlight, to take up a weighty new job may well shake loose some preconceptions about how the world works. (The biographical description applies to Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who has spent most of her adult life in South Bend, Ind., but her ideological commitments appear so strong that she is unlikely to abandon them.)\par \par \uc2 The most interesting theory about Supreme Court ''evolutions'' is suggested by a book that has nothing to do with any court. In ''Private Lives/Public Consequences: Personality and Politics in Modern America,'' a historian, William Chafe, offered portraits of 10 major public figures, including six American presidents and two first ladies. In each case, Professor Chafe trained his narrative lens on a crisis the individuals confronted that jolted them off course and set them on a new path of accomplishment and leadership.\par \par \uc2 I read the book when it was published in 2005 and hadn't gone back to it. But it came to mind last year as I watched Chief Justice Roberts preside over the Senate's charade of an impeachment trial. From all we know about our intensely private chief justice, he has led a charmed life marked by high ambition and great accomplishment. If he has ever been knocked off stride, the world hasn't heard about it.\par \par \uc2 So the analogy with the stories told in ''Private Lives/Public Consequences'' is far from exact. It is, as I said, suggestive: There's certainly no evidence that the impeachment trial or anything else about the Trump years presented \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  with a personal crisis. These four years may not have shattered any notion he had about how government is supposed to work. But did they maybe leave a dent, some shadow of a doubt about the team he played with, the one that brought him success and high office? I'd like to think that's possible. I doubt if there's anyone in Washington more relieved to see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leave the scene than Chief Justice Roberts.\par \par \uc2 That brings me back to the oleaginous Senator Hawley, a Republican from Missouri, and his effort to block the constitutional transfer of power. What does Chief Justice Roberts see when he looks at images of this young man, now contorted by ambition and, I would like to suppose, ruined by it?\par \par \uc2 Does he see an aberration who bears little resemblance to the law clerk who left his chambers about a dozen years ago, one of some 60 clerks who have come and gone over his 15 years as chief justice? Or might he see his former clerk as a metaphor for what happens when people lose sight of the boundaries that keep them and the institutions they serve in their proper roles, when the boundaries themselves dissolve and only the enablers are left to walk the scorched earth?\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAMON WINTER/THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210117eh1h0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex1