\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Justices Revive a Restriction on Obtaining an  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Liptak \par \uc2 1,085 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In their first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case since Justice Amy Coney Barrett joined the court, the justices reinstated a requirement that women seeking medication abortions pick up a pill in person.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- In the Supreme Court's first ruling on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the arrival of Justice Amy Coney Barrett, the court on Tuesday reinstated a federal requirement that women seeking to end their pregnancies using medications pick up a pill in person from a hospital or medical office.\par \par \uc2 The court's brief order was unsigned, and the three more liberal justices dissented. The only member of the majority to offer an explanation was Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 ., who said the ruling was a limited one that deferred to the views of experts.\par \par \uc2 The question, he wrote, was not whether the requirement imposed ''an undue burden on a woman's right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a general matter.'' Instead, he wrote, it was whether a federal judge should have second-guessed the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's determination ''because of the court's own evaluation of the impact of the Covid-19 pandemic.''\par \par \uc2 ''Here as in related contexts concerning government responses to the pandemic,'' the chief justice wrote, quoting an earlier opinion, ''my view is that courts owe significant deference to the politically accountable entities with the 'background, competence and expertise to assess public health.'''\par \par \uc2 In dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , joined by Justice Elena Kagan, said the majority was grievously wrong.\par \par \uc2 ''This country's laws have long singled out abortions for more onerous treatment than other medical procedures that carry similar or greater risks,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote. ''Like many of those laws, maintaining the F.D.A.'s in-person requirements'' for picking up the drug ''during the pandemic not only treats \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  exceptionally, it imposes an unnecessary, irrational and unjustifiable undue burden on women seeking to exercise their right to choose.''\par \par \uc2 She suggested that the next administration should revisit the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''One can only hope that the government will reconsider and exhibit greater care and empathy for women seeking some measure of control over their health and reproductive lives in these unsettling times,'' Justice Sotomayor wrote.\par \par \uc2 Julia Kaye, a lawyer with \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 , said the Supreme Court had taken an extraordinary step.\par \par \uc2 ''The court's ruling rejects science, compassion and decades of legal precedent in service of the Trump administration's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda,'' she said in a statement. ''It is mind-boggling that the Trump administration's top priority on its way out the door is to needlessly endanger even more people during this dark pandemic winter -- and chilling that the Supreme Court allowed it.''\par \par \uc2 Judge Theodore D. Chuang, of the Federal District Court in Maryland, had blocked the requirement in light of the coronavirus pandemic, saying that a needless trip to a medical facility during a health crisis very likely imposed an undue burden on the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The case concerned a restriction on medication abortions, which are permitted in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy. About 60 percent of abortions performed in those weeks use two drugs rather than surgery.\par \par \uc2 The first drug, mifepristone, blocks the effects of progesterone, a hormone without which the lining of the uterus begins to break down. A second drug, misoprostol, taken 24 to 48 hours later, induces contractions of the uterus that expel its contents.\par \par \uc2 The contested measure requires women to appear in person to pick up the mifepristone and to sign a form, even when they had already consulted with their doctors remotely. The women can then take the drug when and where they choose. There is no requirement that women pick up misoprostol in person, and it is available at retail and mail-order pharmacies.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  and other groups, all represented by the A.C.L.U., sued to suspend the requirement that women make a trip to obtain the first drug in light of the pandemic. There was no good reason, the groups said, to require a visit when the drug could be delivered or mailed.\par \par \uc2 Judge Chuang blocked the measure in July, saying that requiring pregnant women, many of them poor, to travel to obtain the drug imposed needless risk and delay, particularly given that the pandemic had forced many clinics to reduce their hours.\par \par \uc2 He imposed a nationwide injunction, reasoning that the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  has more than 60,000 members practicing in all 50 states and that its membership includes some 90 percent of the nation's obstetricians and gynecologists.\par \par \uc2 A unanimous three-judge panel of the United States Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit, in Richmond, Va., refused to stay Judge Chuang's injunction while an appeal moved forward. The Trump administration, which often seeks Supreme Court intervention on an emergency basis when it loses in the lower courts, asked the justices in August to stay the injunction.\par \par \uc2 In October, in its first encounter with the case, the Supreme Court issued an unusual order returning the case to Judge Chuang, saying that ''a more comprehensive record would aid this court's review'' and instructing him to rule within 40 days. In the meantime, the disputed requirement remained suspended.\par \par \uc2 Judge Chuang issued a second opinion on Dec. 9, again blocking the requirement. The ''health risk has only gotten worse,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration returned to the Supreme Court. Its brief focused mainly on data from Indiana and Nebraska, where state laws continued to require women to pick up the pills in person.\par \par \uc2 In those states, the administration told the justices, the number of abortions had increased compared to the previous year. That showed, the administration's brief said, that the requirement did not amount to an unconstitutional burden on the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That argument, lawyers for the medical group wrote in response, ''defies rudimentary principles of statistical analysis.'' Many factors could account for the rise in the number of abortions in the two states during the pandemic, they wrote, including disruptions in access to contraceptives, unemployment and other circumstances ''that have made unwanted pregnancy more likely and parenting less tenable for some.''\par \par \uc2 Justice Sotomayor was also unimpressed by the argument. ''Reading the government's statistically insignificant, cherry-picked data,'' she wrote, ''is no more informative than reading tea leaves.''\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's brief order on Tuesday was unsigned. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANNA MONEYMAKER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210113eh1d0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
