\par\par \uc2 Foreign Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Northern Ireland Is Sued For Lack of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megan Specia \par \uc2 868 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2021\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 7\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2021 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A human rights group says the government's inaction has left a health care void in a country where the procedure was legalized in 2019, but remains largely unavailable.\par \par \uc2 A human rights group in Northern Ireland is taking legal action against the government over its failure to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the group announced on Monday, highlighting the continuing struggle for safe abortions more than a year after the procedure was legalized in the region.\par \par \uc2 The organization, the \uc2 Northern Ireland Human Rights Commission\uc2 , cited deep concerns about a lack of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, which it says has left a health care void for many women and girls.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legalized in Northern Ireland in October 2019, overturning one of the world's most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, but in the months since, women seeking the procedure have faced a number of roadblocks.\par \par \uc2 Les Allamby, the chief commissioner of the independent body behind the lawsuit, said it was important to remember that the issue ''is about a critical health service for women.''\par \par \uc2 ''Our focus is to ensure that human rights are upheld and the law passed in Westminster is implemented in practice,'' said Mr. Allamby, whose group is answerable to \uc2 British Parliament\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The legal action -- a judicial review proceeding filed in Northern Ireland's High Court -- takes aim at the secretary of state, Brandon Lewis; the Northern Ireland Executive; and the Department of Health for Northern Ireland for failing to commission and fund \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 The change to the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law was pushed through in an amendment to a routine bill on governance of Northern Ireland and passed in the \uc2 British Parliament\uc2  in 2019. At the same time, the Parliament legalized same-sex marriage in Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 Legal abortions began in the nation in April 2020, but the procedure has remained largely unavailable, with resistance coming from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and church groups.\par \par \uc2 With the coronavirus ravaging the region, producing the highest number of cases seen since this pandemic began, access has become even more limited. Hospitals are stretched, diverting essential health care workers elsewhere in some cases.\par \par \uc2 This week, one of the five trusts that oversees hospitals in the region closed its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic when a health care provider went on medical leave. Another trust shut its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services from October until Jan. 4 because of a lack of financial resources.\par \par \uc2 Travel restrictions have also exacerbated an already difficult situation, as many women in Northern Ireland have travelled to England to have the procedure. But the risk to their own health and the travel restrictions throughout the last year have made that all but impossible, rights groups say.\par \par \uc2 The region's Department of Health has failed to issue any guidance on how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services should be provided. The department previously issued a statement to the Belfast Telegraph saying it was not required to commission \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services. It said further decisions about commissioning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services would come from the Northern Ireland Executive and Assembly after a public consultation.\par \par \uc2 But the Northern Ireland Executive, the devolved government of the region, received requests from the department in April and May seeking to commission abortions, and has yet to bring forth a debate.\par \par \uc2 Critics say the inaction is an excuse to delay the implementation of a robust program over an issue that remains contentious, with some of the political parties in power still advocating against access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Department of Heath and Northern Ireland Executive did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 But lawmakers in \uc2 British Parliament\uc2  have voiced concern about the issue. The deputy leader of the Alliance Party, Stephen Farry, tweeting on Sunday: ''It shouldn't fall again to the courts to ensure delivery of the law. The law on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now settled.''\par \par \uc2 While the rest of the United Kingdom has made way for telemedicine services for those seeking abortions, Northern Ireland has not, despite the health minister being given specific powers to allow for telemedicine if necessary, which he declined.\par \par \uc2 Alliance for Choice, a Northern Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  group and a vocal critic of the lack of services, voiced its support for the legal action and said the issue has already reached a crisis point for many women and girls.\par \par \uc2 Emma Campbell, the group's co-chairwoman, said that for most women seeking an early-term \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the process is a simple procedure of taking five pills. Her group is providing ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  doulas'' to give advice and support either by telephone or by message.\par \par \uc2 ''We get loads of calls from people who don't know, who are cross and angry and upset,'' Ms. Campbell said.\par \par \uc2 The rights group \uc2 Amnesty International\uc2  also voiced support for the legal action.\par \par \uc2 ''It is appalling that, yet again, litigation is needed to uphold the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  health care in Northern Ireland,'' Patrick Corrigan, the Northern Ireland director, said in a statement. ''Over a year since \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was decriminalized in Northern Ireland and the government's failure to commission and fund services have left women in a vulnerable and dangerous position.''\par \par \uc2 Alliance For Choice activists in Belfast, Northern Ireland. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARY TURNER FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020210112eh1c0004p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
